{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.12.12","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[{"sourceType":"competition","sourceId":129872,"databundleVersionId":15939853}],"dockerImageVersionId":31286,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# CircleID ICDAR 2026 - Pen Classification\n---\n\n**What is this competition?**\n\nWe are given thousands of scanned PNG images of hand-drawn circles. Each circle was drawn by a different person using one of 8 different pens. Our job is to look at a circle image and predict which pen (numbered 1 to 8) was used to draw it.\n\nThink of it like a handwriting expert who can tell just by looking at the ink whether someone used a ballpoint pen, a fountain pen, or a felt-tip marker except here a neural network learns to do that automatically.\n\nThe test set contains circles drawn by writers the model has never seen before. So the model cannot just memorise which writer uses which pen. It must learn the actual visual texture and thickness of each pen's ink stroke.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 1 - Setup and Imports\n\nBefore writing any machine learning code we need to install and import the right tools. Think of this section like unpacking your toolbox before starting a project.\n\n**What each library does in plain English:**\n\n- **torch / torchvision** - The core deep learning engine. It does the maths of training neural networks, runs them on the GPU, and handles tensors (the multi-dimensional arrays that store image data).\n- **timm** - A library full of pretrained neural networks ready to use. Instead of training from scratch (which takes weeks), we download a network already trained on millions of photos and fine-tune it on our circle images.\n- **albumentations** - A fast image augmentation library. Augmentation means randomly modifying training images (flipping, rotating, adjusting brightness) so the model sees more variety and doesn't memorise the training set.\n- **pandas / numpy** - Standard tools for loading CSV files and doing array maths.\n- **sklearn** — Provides the stratified train/validation split and evaluation metrics.\n- **matplotlib / seaborn** - For plotting charts and visualising results.\n- **PIL** - The Python Imaging Library, used to open and inspect image files.\n- **tqdm** - Prints a live progress bar while the model trains.\n\n**Why do we fix random seeds?**\n\nNeural networks start with random weights, and data loading involves random shuffling. If we set the same seed (42) everywhere before running the code, those random choices are always the same. This means the experiment is reproducible: running the notebook twice gives the same result.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Install required packages\nimport subprocess, sys\n\ndef pip_install(*packages):\n    subprocess.check_call(\n        [sys.executable, '-m', 'pip', 'install', '-q', *packages]\n    )\n\npip_install('timm>=0.9.16', 'albumentations>=1.3.1', 'tqdm')\nprint('Libraries installed.')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T15:14:34.036004Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T15:14:34.036437Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T15:14:39.307485Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T15:14:34.036405Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T15:14:39.306517Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Libraries installed.\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":2},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Standard library\nimport os, random, warnings\nfrom pathlib import Path\nwarnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\n\n# Data handling\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Visualisation\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\nfrom PIL import Image\n\n# Machine learning utilities\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.metrics import f1_score, confusion_matrix, ConfusionMatrixDisplay\n\n# Deep learning core\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.optim as optim\nfrom torch.utils.data import Dataset, DataLoader\nfrom torch.cuda.amp import GradScaler, autocast\nimport timm\n\n# Augmentation library\nimport albumentations as A\nfrom albumentations.pytorch import ToTensorV2\n\n# Progress bars\nfrom tqdm.auto import tqdm\n\nprint(f'PyTorch      : {torch.__version__}')\nprint(f'timm         : {timm.__version__}')\nprint(f'Albumentations: {A.__version__}')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T15:15:41.963042Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T15:15:41.963986Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T15:15:57.690506Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T15:15:41.963935Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T15:15:57.689616Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"PyTorch      : 2.9.0+cpu\ntimm         : 1.0.24\nAlbumentations: 2.0.8\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":3},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Global configuration** - I define every important number in one place. This is good practice: if you want to try 30 epochs instead of 20, you change one line here rather than hunting through the whole notebook.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# All tunable settings live here \nSEED         = 42        # Fixed seed for reproducibility\nIMG_SIZE     = 224       # Every image is resized to 224 x 224 pixels\nBATCH_SIZE   = 32        # 32 images processed together per training step\nNUM_EPOCHS   = 20        # Number of full passes through the training data\nLR           = 3e-4      # Starting learning rate\nWEIGHT_DECAY = 1e-2      # Regularisation penalty on large weights\nNUM_CLASSES  = 8         # Eight pens = eight classes\nLABEL_SMOOTH = 0.1       # Prevents the model from being overconfident\nMODEL_NAME   = 'efficientnetv2_s'  # Pretrained backbone from timm\nNUM_WORKERS  = 2         # Background CPU processes that pre-load images\nTTA_STEPS    = 5         # Number of augmented views used at test time\n\n# Where the competition data lives \n# On Kaggle the path is /kaggle/input/<dataset-name>.\n# If you run locally, point DATA_DIR to your own folder.\nDATA_DIR = Path('/kaggle/input/competitions/icdar-2026-circleid-pen-classification')\nif not DATA_DIR.exists():\n    DATA_DIR = Path('.')   # Fallback: current working directory\n    print(f'[INFO] DATA_DIR set to: {DATA_DIR.resolve()}')\n\nCHECKPOINT  = Path('best_model.pth')    # Where to save the best model\nSUBMIT_PATH = Path('submission_pen.csv') # Final submission file\n\n# Detect GPU \n# If a GPU is available we use it. Otherwise training falls back to CPU,\n# which is much slower but still works for testing the pipeline.\nDEVICE = torch.device('cuda' if torch.cuda.is_available() else 'cpu')\nprint(f'Device: {DEVICE}')\nif DEVICE.type == 'cuda':\n    print(f'GPU   : {torch.cuda.get_device_name(0)}')\n\n# Fix all random seeds\n# We lock the seed in Python, NumPy, and PyTorch so that every random\n# choice (weight initialisation, batch shuffling, augmentations) is the\n# same every time this notebook is run from top to bottom.\ndef seed_everything(seed: int = SEED):\n    random.seed(seed)\n    os.environ['PYTHONHASHSEED'] = str(seed)\n    np.random.seed(seed)\n    torch.manual_seed(seed)\n    torch.cuda.manual_seed_all(seed)\n    torch.backends.cudnn.deterministic = True\n    torch.backends.cudnn.benchmark     = False\n\nseed_everything(SEED)\nprint(f'Seed {SEED} fixed across Python / NumPy / PyTorch.')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T15:19:46.777840Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T15:19:46.778218Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T15:19:46.790258Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T15:19:46.778189Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T15:19:46.789084Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Device: cpu\nSeed 42 fixed across Python / NumPy / PyTorch.\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":7},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 2 - Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)\n\nBefore building any model it is essential to look at the data. EDA stands for Exploratory Data Analysis — it just means 'take a careful look before you start'. A few minutes of EDA prevents hours of debugging later.\n\n**What I want to find out:**\n\n1. Are the eight pen classes evenly represented, or is one pen rare? If class 3 has 10x more images than class 7, the model will be biased toward predicting class 3 and we need to account for that.\n2. What do the actual circle images look like? Are the scans clean or noisy? Do circles from different pens look visually different?\n3. Are all images the same size? Most neural networks need a fixed input size, so knowing the range of image dimensions helps us choose the resize target.\n\nWe start by simply loading the CSV files and printing their shapes.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Load all three CSV files\ntrain_df     = pd.read_csv(DATA_DIR / 'train.csv')\nadd_train_df = pd.read_csv(DATA_DIR / 'additional_train.csv')\ntest_df      = pd.read_csv(DATA_DIR / 'test.csv')\n\nprint('train.csv          shape:', train_df.shape)\nprint('additional_train   shape:', add_train_df.shape)\nprint('test.csv           shape:', test_df.shape)\nprint()\nprint('--- First 5 rows of train.csv ---')\ndisplay(train_df.head())\nprint('--- First 5 rows of additional_train.csv ---')\ndisplay(add_train_df.head())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-09T15:20:16.801986Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-09T15:20:16.803313Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-09T15:20:16.881481Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-09T15:20:16.803275Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-09T15:20:16.880505Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"std